# Cover Letter Standards

## Source of truth

After setup, `design/` should contain **one** working resume and **one** working cover letter (for example `resume.html` and `cover-letter.html`). Those files are the only design sources.

Per-application cover letters under `submissions/` must clone **layout, typography, color tokens, and structural CSS** from that working cover letter. Do not invent a new visual system, pull styles from unused samples, or restyle for the company.

## Non-negotiable design

- Match the working cover letter’s accent, fonts, and light/dark behavior; do not introduce a different palette or type stack
- No company accent colors or logos (keep the logo slot empty unless the user explicitly asks otherwise)
- Dark header / letter-body contrast as in the working source
- No images unless the working source already uses them and the user asks to keep that pattern

## CSS branding block

Keep the per-application branding variables from the working cover letter (names may match this shape):

```css
:root {
  --company-name:        "Company";
  --company-team:        "Team Name";
  --hiring-manager:      "Hiring Manager";
  --role-title:          "Role Title";
  --company-logo-url:    "";
}
```

Edit values for the role; do not change the visual theme tokens when applying.

## Structure

Header → addressee block → role chip → salutation → body (3–5 paragraphs, one pull quote) → signature. Preserve the same section order and components as the working cover letter. Keep the `?continuous` script if the source has it (needed for single-page PDF export).

## Tone

- Open with a specific story or insight from `.career-pipeline.yml` `background.stories`, not a generic introduction
- Acknowledge gaps briefly; do not minimize or dwell
- Close with the canonical invitation: “I'd welcome the opportunity to further illustrate how I can add value to the team at &lt;Company&gt;.” (confidence + offer to prove it). Website link is optional; omit when the user asks.
- Must read as a human wrote it:
  - **No em dashes** (`—`). Use a period, comma, colon, or rewrite the sentence.
  - Favor plain, spoken rhythm over polished AI cadence
  - Avoid stock transitions and filler (“What draws me…”, empty “welcome the opportunity to discuss…”, “through-line”, “leverage”, mirrored three-beat lists). The approved closing above is the exception.
  - Do not rewrite the job description back at them; show how *you* have done the work
  - Vary sentence length; avoid symmetric paragraph patterns
